    "title": "Michigan VA hosts annual veteran recognition ceremony - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Karina Prieto ,\n4 hours ago\nLANSING, Mich. (WLNS) – Wednesday morning, the Michigan Veterans Affairs Agency hosted its annual recognition ceremony, including a special appearance by Governor Gretchen Whitmer and a Vietnam veteran pinning ceremony.\nSix Vietnam veterans received pins of recognition from Whitmer. 6 News spoke with people there about the importance of appreciating the 400,000 Michigan men and women who served in the Vietnam War.\nWhitmer attaching pins to the lapels of 6 Vietnam veterans. (WLNS) “They’ve paid the price, they shed the blood, they spent the time, they lost the comrades,” said Inetta Smith, widow of a Vietnam veteran.\nVeterans and loved ones gathered Wednesday morning, honoring valiant service and great sacrifice.\n“He went through a lot. He saw a lot,” said Smith.\nInetta and Carnet Smith. Smith was there in remembrance of her late husband, Carnet Smith, who served in the U.S. Army and Air Force in Vietnam.\n“You can not like the war. You can not like the conflict, but don’t blame the soldier,” said Smith.\nLike many Vietnam-era vets, Smith says her husband did not get a warm welcome home and was left with PTSD and struggles with addiction.\nBarry Walter. (WLNS) “There have been times in the past where you wonder, ‘Am I appreciated?’ We know that we are, but it’s good to have that affirmation,” said Vietnam Army veteran Barry Walter.\nWalter says Wednesday’s recognition is a great honor, but also an important reminder to thank those who serve.\n“We longed for a long time for the recognition that we have received. They should not have to wait,” said Walter.\nThe event. (WLNS) Smith had the honor of singing the Star-Spangled Banner, a way to connect not only with her late husband, but her father and son who have also served.\n“It is a story of growth, a story of redemption, a story of ‘we’re going to make it, we can see the flag through the bombs bursting in air’,” said Smith.\nThe pins given to the veterans are engraved with the words “A grateful nation thanks and honors you”, meant to be worn close to the heart of each veteran.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WLNS 6 News.\n14",
